 The completion-steal arm, shaped exactly like the flight that found
 it: a combo device carries keyboard and mouse endpoints on ONE slot,
 the keyboard is bound but never typed on, and both pumps run in the
 same loop the desktop runs them in. A silent keyboard pump polls its
 slot on every iteration, so under a latch keyed by slot alone it
 consumes the mouse's completions, the mouse pump never leaves its
 armed phase, and the pointer freezes after the first theft -- the
 keyboard-shaped interfaces of a Logitech Unifying receiver did this
 to the mouse on real silicon on 2026-08-04. Keyed by slot AND
 endpoint, every sample folds no matter which pump drains the ring.

Section: Test

 The wait is bounded by the RTC rather than by an iteration count: each
 pump costs several VM exits, so a count large enough to cover the
 timeline in iterations is minutes of wall clock.

  uhs-wait : UsbKbd, UsbMouse, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  uhs-wait (k) (m) (x) (y) (prev) (secs) = act
    let p = kbd-pump k
    in let q = mouse-pump m 1024 768
    in act
      now <- rtc-seconds 0
      if if mouse-x m == x then mouse-y m == y else False then 1
      else if secs <= 0 then 0
      else if now < 0 then uhs-wait k m x y prev secs
      else if now == prev then uhs-wait k m x y prev secs
      else uhs-wait k m x y now (secs - 1)
    end
  end

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Nothing = act
    att <- usb-attach
    let k = att.ua-kbd
    in let m = att.ua-mouse
    in act
      print-line-uni ("kbd: ok=" & (if k.uk-ok then "1" else "0") & " mouse: ok=" & (if m.um-ok then "1" else "0") & " same-dev=" & (if (k.uk-dev).ud-slot == (m.um-dev).ud-slot then "1" else "0"))
      s0 <- rtc-seconds 0
      hit <- uhs-wait k m 80 40 s0 10
      print-line-uni ("pos=" & show (mouse-x m) & "," & show (mouse-y m) & " btn=" & show (mouse-buttons m) & " hit=" & show hit)
    end
  end
